WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.419 --> 00:00:13.362
Fix introduced himself at the Bombay police headquarters.

2
00:00:15.303 --> 00:00:18.986
He had hurried there first thing in the morning, as soon as the Mongolia had docked.

3
00:00:20.628 --> 00:00:25.071
He inquired if the warrant for Fogg's arrest had arrived from London yet, but it had not.

4
00:00:25.091 --> 00:00:32.053
"If the Mongolia were not two days ahead of schedule, I wouldn't be in this situation," Fix explained

5
00:00:32.113 --> 00:00:33.713
to the Bombay director of police.

6
00:00:35.554 --> 00:00:38.596
He urged the director to issue an order for the arrest of Mr.

7
00:00:38.656 --> 00:00:40.537
Fogg, but the director refused.

8
00:00:41.958 --> 00:00:47.661
"The matter concerns the London office," the director explained, "and only London can deliver the order."

9
00:00:49.022 --> 00:00:51.243
Fix fumed, but he did not insist.

10
00:00:51.263 --> 00:00:58.567
"Bombay is far enough from London so that a fleeing bank robber will not worry about being seen,"

11
00:00:58.947 --> 00:00:59.428
he thought.

12
00:01:00.396 --> 00:01:00.856
So Mr.

13
00:01:00.876 --> 00:01:05.799
Fogg would probably stay in Bombay for a while, and the warrant would arrive before his departure.

14
00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:10.842
In the meantime Fix was determined not to lose sight of his mysterious suspect.

15
00:01:11.643 --> 00:01:13.744
He hurried through the crowded streets back to the boat.

16
00:01:15.125 --> 00:01:16.406
He wanted to be there when Mr.

17
00:01:16.446 --> 00:01:17.306
Fogg disembarked.

18
00:01:18.667 --> 00:01:21.669
Mr. Fogg, for his part, left the steamer in the afternoon.

19
00:01:21.909 --> 00:01:27.052
After saying good-bye to his whist partners, he descended the gangway, carrying the carpetbag in his hand.

20
00:01:28.328 --> 00:01:33.172
On the shore he instructed Passepartout to attend to several errands, but urged him to be at the

21
00:01:33.212 --> 00:01:35.554
train station at eight o'clock and not a minute late.

22
00:01:37.256 --> 00:01:40.478
Then, with steps as regular as the ticking of a watch, Mr.

23
00:01:40.518 --> 00:01:42.260
Fogg walked to the passport office.

24
00:01:43.781 --> 00:01:48.085
After receiving a visa, he turned his steps to the train station, where he planned to have dinner

25
00:01:48.245 --> 00:01:50.126
and wait for the eight o'clock train to Calcutta.

26
00:01:51.468 --> 00:01:56.492
Passepartout, like the detective, had thought his master would stay in Bombay at least for a few days.

27
00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:02.284
When he heard his master's orders to meet at the train station, he realized that they were going

28
00:02:02.324 --> 00:02:03.645
to continue without delay.

29
00:02:04.927 --> 00:02:10.132
He purchased the shirts and shoes his master had requested and took a leisurely stroll through the strange

30
00:02:10.232 --> 00:02:11.153
sights of Bombay.

31
00:02:12.594 --> 00:02:21.062
Many nationalities crowded the streets: Persians with pointed caps, long-robed Armenians, Indians wearing turbans, and many others in

32
00:02:21.122 --> 00:02:22.784
bright robes of red and orange.

33
00:02:24.412 --> 00:02:29.514
In a market he saw a procession of dancers clothed in rose- colored gowns looped with gold and

34
00:02:29.574 --> 00:02:34.695
silver. They danced airily through the streets to the sound of tambourines.

35
00:02:36.336 --> 00:02:38.876
Passepartout had never seen anything like them before.

36
00:02:40.477 --> 00:02:43.678
Passepartout followed the dancers through winding streets and lanes.

37
00:02:45.958 --> 00:02:50.360
After a while he realized that his curiosity had led him farther from the center of the city

38
00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:51.220
than he had intended.

39
00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:57.924
When he turned back to head to the train station, he noticed a magnificent Indian temple on nearby

40
00:02:57.984 --> 00:02:58.804
Malabar Hill.

41
00:03:00.505 --> 00:03:05.228
It was too splendid to pass up, and he climbed the hill, seized with a desire to see

42
00:03:05.268 --> 00:03:05.929
its interior.

43
00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:13.754
Passepartout, however, did not realize that it was forbidden for Christians to enter certain Indian temples, and that

44
00:03:13.874 --> 00:03:16.296
even the faithful must leave their shoes outside.

45
00:03:17.697 --> 00:03:22.460
The British government severely punished anyone who disregarded the practices of India's religions.

46
00:03:23.668 --> 00:03:28.871
Meaning no harm, Passepartout went in like a simple tourist and was soon lost in admiration of the

47
00:03:28.931 --> 00:03:30.352
incredible ornamentation.

48
00:03:30.372 --> 00:03:34.534
"I have never seen anything so magnificent!"

49
00:03:34.854 --> 00:03:38.076
he was thinking when suddenly he was knocked to the stone floor.

50
00:03:39.557 --> 00:03:40.057
He looked up.

51
00:03:41.478 --> 00:03:44.539
Three angry priests jumped on him and tore off his shoes.

52
00:03:45.540 --> 00:03:47.681
They began to beat him, exclaiming loudly.

53
00:03:47.701 --> 00:03:51.583
"Help, help, they are stealing my shoes, someone help me!"

54
00:03:51.963 --> 00:03:54.416
screamed Passepartout.

55
00:03:54.436 --> 00:03:59.158
He got to his feet again and wasted no time in knocking down two of the priests before

56
00:03:59.238 --> 00:04:02.219
rushing out of the pagoda as fast as his legs would carry him.

57
00:04:03.180 --> 00:04:07.401
When Passepartout finally arrived at the station, he was hatless and shoeless.

58
00:04:08.862 --> 00:04:12.904
In the fight he had also lost the package with the newly purchased shirts and shoes.

59
00:04:14.524 --> 00:04:16.245
It was five minutes before eight o'clock.

60
00:04:17.565 --> 00:04:18.626
Fix had followed Mr.

61
00:04:18.666 --> 00:04:23.788
Fogg and was on the platform, having decided to follow him to Calcutta and farther, if necessary.

62
00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:30.304
Listening from behind a newspaper, he heard Passepartout explain his adventure to his master.

63
00:04:31.464 --> 00:04:37.509
Phileas Fogg was very annoyed by Passepartout's misadventure, but all he said was, "I hope that this will

64
00:04:37.549 --> 00:04:38.329
not happen again."

65
00:04:39.410 --> 00:04:41.431
He turned his back and stepped onto the train.

66
00:04:42.072 --> 00:04:44.293
Passepartout followed his master without a word.

67
00:04:45.854 --> 00:04:49.097
Fix was about to enter a different train car when an idea struck him.

68
00:04:49.117 --> 00:04:51.719
"I'll stay!"

69
00:04:52.139 --> 00:04:53.440
announced Fix to himself.

70
00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:57.121
"An offense has been committed on Indian soil.

71
00:04:58.681 --> 00:04:59.802
I've got my man!"

72
00:05:01.122 --> 00:05:06.123
Just then the locomotive gave a sharp screech, and the train moved into the darkness of the Indian

73
00:05:06.183 --> 00:05:12.565
night. Well, I learned my lesson about visiting temples in India.

74
00:05:13.986 --> 00:05:17.407
Next time I travel, I'll be sure to learn more about local customs first!

75
00:05:18.787 --> 00:05:21.748
In India most people practice a religion called Hinduism.

76
00:05:23.052 --> 00:05:28.036
There are many different forms of Hinduism, but most Hindus believe in a spiritual entity called Brahman.

77
00:05:29.558 --> 00:05:32.160
Brahman is the supreme universal spirit in Hinduism.

78
00:05:33.081 --> 00:05:35.222
Hindus worship in a mandir or temple.

79
00:05:36.684 --> 00:05:41.608
Hinduism contains many distinct deities, so different mandirs are dedicated to different deities.

80
00:05:43.189 --> 00:05:44.851
It's not just people who visit these temples.

81
00:05:45.932 --> 00:05:50.856
Hindus believe all life should be treated with respect so animals are often seen hanging around too!

82
00:05:51.744 --> 00:05:54.909
When you go inside a mandir, you must remember to be respectful.

83
00:05:55.590 --> 00:05:59.375
This includes keeping your hands folded and removing your shoes before you enter.

84
00:06:00.376 --> 00:06:01.778
Oh, how I wish I had known that one!
